Runner      : .NET 10.0.300 SDK present (dotnet --info → 10.0.300; WinUI x64 toolchain builds) — NOT blocked.

=== APPROACH ===
- The repository's WinUI app is code-first C# (no .xaml page files); every feature surface lives under
  apps/windows/TeslaSync.App/feature-views/<Name>/ as <Name>.Model.cs (pure, Microsoft.UI-free projection,
  unit-tested headlessly), <Name>.Source.cs (the generated-client feed), <Name>.ViewModel.cs
  (INotifyPropertyChanged + IDisposable state holder) and <Name>.cs (the Fluent UserControl view), exactly as the
  sibling W7 EnergyFlowPage does. The generated prompt's idealized "PowerFlowDashboardPage.xaml +
  PowerFlowDashboardPageViewModel.cs under TeslaSync/Features/Battery" is mapped onto these real conventions; the
  surface registers itself with the W4 ShellPageFactory by route name ("PowerFlowDashboard", already in
  RouteTable.cs L280 → /power-flow, group BatteryEnergy) via ShellWindow.xaml.cs.
- Data binding (ADR-004): the three manifest data sources are the web hooks useTeslaEnergyLiveStatus,
  useTeslaEnergyLiveStatusHistory and useRefreshTeslaEnergyLiveStatus. PowerFlowClientFeed
  (PowerFlowDashboardPage.Source.cs) binds to the generated OpenAPI contract client operations
  get_api_v1_tesla_energy_sites_siteID_live_status (path siteID), get_api_v1_tesla_energy_sites_siteID_live_status_history
  (path siteID + snake_case since/until/limit query, matching the web hook) and
  post_api_v1_tesla_energy_sites_siteID_live_status_refresh (path siteID). All three operation ids resolve against
  the generated endpoint table (Generated/Api/ApiEndpoints.cs L475/L476/L477) — asserted by
  ClientFeed_operations_resolve_against_the_generated_endpoint_table + the FakeApiClient request-shaping tests. The
  descriptors declare zero query params, so GeneratedApiClient.BuildQuery permits the trailing since/until/limit
  params (allowed.Count==0 path). The default PowerFlowDashboardPage() ctor uses EmptyPowerFlowFeed (the empty data
  state), mirroring EnergyFlowPage; the client feed is exercised by tests. No HTTP touches the view.
- The live-status query is the PRIMARY source that drives the page state. The backend returns either a real
  snapshot (with an `id`) or a {message:…} notice when nothing has been fetched yet — the web reads this as
  hasLiveData = liveStatus && 'id' in liveStatus, reproduced by PowerFlowLiveReading.FromJson (HasData = the JSON
  has an `id` member). The history query is a BEST-EFFORT overlay independent of the page state: a history failure
  leaves the two charts on their empty body, never erroring the page (web hooks are independent;
  ViewModel_history_failure_does_not_error_the_page). Refresh POSTs a fresh snapshot then, on success, re-reads both
  queries (web onSuccess invalidation; ViewModel_refresh_posts_then_reloads_through_the_feed).
- These data sources are REST queries (the web uses TanStack refetchInterval polling, NOT an SSE stream), so no SSE
  client is wired (ADR-009/S4 applies only to live SSE surfaces). Freshness (ADR-013) is surfaced on the "Updated:"
  badge: PowerFlowProjection.IsStale flags a snapshot older than 2 minutes and tints the badge Warning instead of
  Neutral (Last_update_badge_is_warning_when_snapshot_is_stale).
- The web renders its states inside the PageContainer body, so the native port reproduces them as visible Fluent
  regions per ADR-006: loading → TsStatGridSkeleton(4) + TsChartBlockSkeleton shimmer; error → TsQueryError
  (InfoBar-equivalent) + Retry; empty/success → the always-visible scaffold (header, refresh, four status chips,
  four current-power tiles, battery-state panel, power-flow diagram, two charts). In the empty data state the
  battery/flow panels render their own TsEmptyState ("No battery data — refresh to fetch" / "No power flow data
  yet") and the tiles show the em dash, exactly as the web composes it; the two charts cover a missing history with
  TsChartContainer.State = Empty/Loading. Never a blank region.
- SI units (ADR-002): all DB/API/struct/TS values are SI (watts, watt-hours). The web page formats power/energy at
  the render boundary with its own magnitude-adaptive fmtWatts / fmtWh helpers (W below 1 kW, kW above; Wh below
  1 kWh, kWh above) — reproduced verbatim in PowerFlowProjection.FormatWatts / FormatWattHours via
  NumberFormatting.Format (the fmtNumber port), pinned by FormatWatts_matches_web + FormatWattHours_matches_web.
  SOC is fmtNumber(soc,1)+"%". History timestamps render through DateTimeFormatting (formatDateShort/formatDateTime
  ports).
- i18n (ADR-014): this web page uses dotted i18next keys (t('powerFlow.title'), …). All 33 manifest strings flow
  through ILocalizer.GetString("powerFlow.<key>", "<English default>") in PowerFlowProjection.Project, resolved
  UNCONDITIONALLY at the top of Project so every key is recorded in every data state (tests
  Projection_resolves_every_required_string_key_in_success + _in_the_loading_state + _in_the_empty_state). 31 of the
  33 keys already existed in Strings/{en,ar,he}/Resources.resw; the 2 chart-aria keys (powerFlow.powerOverTime.aria,
  powerFlow.socOverTime.aria) are NOT in web en.json (the web relies on i18next inline-default fallback for them,
  which ILocalizer.GetString reproduces). To satisfy the "every visible literal sourced from .resw" criterion I
  added those 2 keys verbatim (web key names, "translation.powerFlow.*.aria") to all three catalogs in JS-ordinal
  sorted position (LF/UTF-8-no-BOM preserved). NOTE: the resw is generator-owned and sources from web en.json; these
  2 hand-added keys would not be re-emitted by gen-i18n until the same keys land in web en.json — documented here,
  not silent. The resw is not part of the dotnet build, so this does not affect the build gate.
- The four current-power tiles use the shared TsStatCard (web StatCard: accent glyph + label + value + state
  sub-line) — its Sublabel carries the Charging/Discharging/Importing/Exporting state word. The SOC gauge bar uses
  the shared TsMetricBar (web gradient progress bar). The flow arrows (web FlowArrow) render as tokenized Border
  chips with an up/down direction glyph (web ArrowUp/ArrowDown) dimmed when inactive. Charts use the W3 native
  wrappers (never a web canvas): TsAreaChart (power-over-time, four overlaid SI-watt series) and TsLineChart (SOC),
  both inside a TsChartContainer with title/subtitle/AccessibleSummary + DataView for screen-reader access.
- a11y (ADR-015): AutomationProperties.Name on the page (Group peer), every panel, both charts (the .aria strings),
  the error/empty surfaces and the flow chips; the refresh button and Retry are keyboard-focusable TsButtons; the
  light/dark/high-contrast token brushes drive all color (no hardcoded brushes).
- Out of scope: the web RangePicker on the history header is not a manifest parity item; the native port computes a
  fixed trailing 7-day window (web preset '7d') via PowerFlowProjection.WindowSince/WindowUntil with limit=1000,
  preserving the since/until/limit data-source contract without adding an out-of-scope interactive control.

